<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coxen Hole</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coxen Hole is the main town of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a> and capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. Settled by English-speaking islanders in the 19th century, it was the seat of the British Colony of the Bay Islands from 1852 to 1860. It is now the island's administrative centre and a cruise-ship port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coxen-hole.jpg" alt="Coxen Hole" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Larry D. Moore · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Roatan_Coxen_Hole_Roatan_Honduras_2026.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Coxen Hole lies on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a> off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, and is the largest town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. The island's Juan Manuel Gálvez International Airport is located there. English settlement on Roatán is older than the town: records show Port Royal on the island occupied again in 1742, when the British tried to gain possession of most of the Caribbean coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and rebuilt the old fort there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The archives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a> record a Major Caulfield in command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a> as early as 1745. On 2 August that year he wrote to Edward Trelawny, Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, describing Spanish harassment of the English settlements, which appear to have been well established on the island by 1775. The town itself was founded in 1835, when several British families moved to Roatán from the Cayman Islands. It takes its name from the pirate Captain John Coxen; in the 17th century Roatán was home to over 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>.</p><p>The Caymanian slave-owners brought their slaves and their seafaring culture to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roatan">Roatán</a>, and these settlers came to form most of the island's permanent population. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a> passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> through the Wyke-Cruz Treaty, the British settlers took Honduran nationality. The fruit trade of the late 1800s, the era of the so-called banana republics, increased the American and European presence in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Coxen Hole had a population of 8,307 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Various dialects of English remain the main language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a>. Buses connect the town with other places on the island, and it has a stadium and a small hospital, the Woods Medical Facility.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coxen Hole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony was small, with a few thousand inhabitants living by fishing, turtling, coconuts and fruit trade with New Orleans, and was run by a lieutenant governor and local council at <b>Coxen Hole</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-the-bay-islands">Colony of the Bay Islands</a></li></ul></section>
